WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.149 --> 00:00:08.440
The vast oceans of the video game Subnautica
and its sequel Below Zero teem with incredible

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:17.190
life — some friendly, and some deadly. While
the series is focused on survival, the lifeforms

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:23.769
of Subnautica are amazing even when they’re
trying to eat you: possessing fascinating

00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:29.930
biology, bizarre behavioral patterns, and
hidden mysteries. I’ve explored the various

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:36.010
creatures of the series before on my channel,
and in this special compilation episode — you

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:42.400
can get the full, documentary-style experience
in one video. From the sunlit shallows to

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.940
the deepest abyss, we’ll explore the biology
behind these puzzling aliens, and discover

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:55.440
the role they play in their larger ecosystems.
So, let’s take the plunge together, and

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:03.370
see what awaits us under the surface… Beginning
in the safety of shallow warm waters, we find

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:09.290
that stable conditions have led to a region
crowded with varied life. One of the first

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:15.000
lifeforms we can spot are the Peepers… or
rather, they spot us, with their enormous,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.530
dinner-plate like eyes. These easily identifiable
creatures have evolved visual sensors capable

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:27.800
of discerning colors not just in clear waters
but in a variety of lighting conditions. This,

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.479
combined with their surprising speed, means
that despite being herbivores, Peepers aren’t

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:38.960
at the bottom of the food chain. That distinction
instead belongs to these creatures, the rather

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:45.240
unfortunate looking Garryfish. Docile herbivores
that are remarkably slow, the Garryfish make

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.799
easy prey for most anything else in these
shallow waters. All ecosystems, including

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:55.590
the oceans of earth, need something near the
bottom of the food chain, after all. Other

00:01:55.590 --> 00:02:01.810
common herbivores on 4546B include the Bladderfish,
which swims by contracting and expanding its

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:08.350
translucent bladder, and the unusual Boomerang,
a horizontally symmetrical oddity that possesses

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:15.099
a mouth full of sharp teeth to break down
coral. On the subject of coral, the landscape

00:02:15.099 --> 00:02:22.080
of the Safe Shallows is dominated by one particularly
unusual lifeform. These gargantuan tubes are

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:30.800
large enough to swim through, and despite
appearances, are very much alive. Aptly called

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.859
Giant Coral Tubes, they don’t look much
like the coral we know on earth. These tubes

00:02:35.859 --> 00:02:40.470
have evolved to filter nutrients that flow
through their center, and judging by their

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:47.010
size and abundance, it’s a strategy that
has been quite successful. Gliding near the

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:53.680
tubes is the elegant Rabbit Ray — the first
of many ray species we may encounter. The

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.599
undulating motion that the Rabbit Ray uses
to swim through the clear seas is analogous

00:02:58.599 --> 00:03:04.000
to the underwater movements of various types
of earth rays. But it’s not the movement

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.459
of the Rabbit Ray that gives it its curious
name. Instead, the Rabbit Ray’s most defining

00:03:09.459 --> 00:03:16.989
features are its twin orange appendages that
help it sense vibrations. These ear-like structures

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:21.069
tangentially resemble the ears of rabbits,
and so the aquatic Rabbit Ray shares a name

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:28.790
with an otherwise dissimilar terrestrial herbivore.
Beneath the shallows twist a network of underwater

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:36.260
caves. While exploring one as I gathered this
footage, I heard a strange noise… And a

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:43.069
fish exploded in my face. That short-lived
creature was a Crashfish — an evolutionary

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:48.209
oddity that is difficult to study due to its
highly frustrating habit of exploding when

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:50.549
anything gets close. The Crashfish is defending
the sulfur plant, that provides the Crashfish

00:03:50.549 --> 00:03:54.859
its home. While an animal exploding to defend
its territory might seem too outlandish to

00:03:54.859 --> 00:03:59.481
evolve, there’s a model of such behavior
on our own planet. Though uncommon, certain

00:03:59.481 --> 00:04:04.609
species of ants have been observed erupting
into goo to block tunnels when their nest

00:04:04.609 --> 00:04:13.450
is under threat. Truly, nature is highly unusual
— no matter the planet. Elsewhere in the

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:19.160
caves, the far less bothersome Shuttlebugs
drift near the stone walls. Shuttlebugs are

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:24.270
challenging creatures to make sense of: with
three legs and three mandibles that have evolved

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:30.160
into fin-like tentacles. Despite looking slightly
unsettling, analysis suggests Shuttlebugs

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:35.300
are too small to be a threat to most organisms,
and have instead adapted to feed on the waste

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:42.449
products of the ecosystem around them. Leaving
the caves, you’ll see our first large species

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:51.280
is waiting for us: the Gasopods. These rotund,
leathery beasts resemble Earth manatees, yet

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:56.550
have a curious defense that is all their own.
Although not in any way hostile, when threatened,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:05:02.330
the Gasopods will nonetheless release a deadly
cloud of underwater mist… which I can’t

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:07.870
recommend ingesting. Chemical analysis of
the Gasopods tells us the poisonous compound

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:14.280
comes from the bulbous appendage at the end
of their tail. Since Gasopods are social creatures,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:20.520
it’s possible they even use these clouds
to communicate with one another. But while

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:25.030
the Gasopods are the largest creatures in
the Safe Shallows, they are barely the tip

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:31.720
of the proverbial iceberg when it comes to
planet 4546B. Venturing into the vast Kelp

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:36.439
Forest, we’ll find a radically different
environment than the region we just left.

00:05:36.439 --> 00:05:41.860
Here, the light is low thanks to the high
volume of creepvine plants — creating a

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:48.419
hidden ecosystem, just like kelp-heavy regions
in Earth’s oceans. Here and there, clusters

00:05:48.419 --> 00:05:54.720
of Creepvine Seeds cast shadows across the
environment like natural floodlights. I find

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:03.660
myself wondering just what might be hidden
among the swaying fronds… That is a Stalker:

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:09.759
the largest predator that makes its home in
the dense Creepvines. These fearsome customers

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:15.650
possess a series of dorsal ridges to help
them make tight turns. Their long, thin snout

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:21.500
is likely adapted for ensnaring fish, as it
greatly resembles the effective, slender snout

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:27.979
of real-life gharials. Yet at the edge of
the Kelp Forest, one Stalker is behaving…

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:34.870
rather oddly. This Stalker is picking up various
pieces of metal scrap with its snout, only

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:41.069
to drop them after a short period. This behavior
seems to serve no practical purpose, and indeed

00:06:41.069 --> 00:06:46.590
the stalkers seem to simply have a fondness
for shiny objects. Puzzling as this behavior

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:52.180
might seem, the barracuda — a predatory
earth fish — is also known to be attracted

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:56.770
to shiny objects because it’s evolved to
spot the silvery glint of the fish it prefers

00:06:56.770 --> 00:07:02.539
to prey on. And when one considers the common
prey of the Stalker, this attraction to shiny

00:07:02.539 --> 00:07:08.659
materials makes perfect sense. In the murky
depths of the Kelp Forest, schools of fast-moving

00:07:08.659 --> 00:07:14.219
hoopfish light up the dark with their vibrant
coloring. A brief study of hoopfish anatomy

00:07:14.219 --> 00:07:19.490
reveals they get their name from their antennae,
which extends from the top and bottom of their

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:24.610
heads and bends around their tails — forming
a hoop. Exactly why these stunning fish possess

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:30.879
such intense and varied colors, however, is
a mystery. On the subject of mysteries, the

00:07:30.879 --> 00:07:36.729
last fish of the Kelp Forest we’ll see is
quite the enigma. Meet the Hoverfish: a peaceful

00:07:36.729 --> 00:07:41.810
herbivore that appears to ‘hover’ on the
pads at the ends of its six legs. Using these

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:47.210
strange appendages, the Hoverfish glides on
the undersea currents in a manner quite dissimilar

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:52.289
to any other fish on the planet. It’s surprising
to learn, therefore, that the hoverfish may

00:07:52.289 --> 00:07:58.601
actually be a distant relative of the nondescript
bladder-fish, with their ‘legs’ a highly

00:07:58.601 --> 00:08:03.879
derived form of the bladderfish’s primitive
vacuole tubing. It’s the kind of creature

00:08:03.879 --> 00:08:11.889
that could only exist in the Kelp Forest.
Just beyond the waving Creepvines, the landscape

00:08:11.889 --> 00:08:18.340
gives way to an alien Grassy Plateau. Here,
the sandy terrain is almost completely carpeted

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:24.509
with Blood Grass, as the seafloor is too deep
for Creepvines to take hold. And in the open

00:08:24.509 --> 00:08:30.979
space, even larger lifeforms are on the prowl…
At the edge of the Grassy Plateau, a strange

00:08:30.979 --> 00:08:36.590
cloud of sand is gathering. Be cautious—as
we swim closer to the unexpected turbulence,

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:43.830
a creature with a huge, fanged mouth lashes
out. This is a Sand Shark, a predator with

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:50.120
a distinctive hunting strategy. The Sand Shark
burrows into the sand, then erupts out when

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:56.850
unsuspecting prey swims by: ending the chase
with a snap from its massive jaws. With segmented

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:02.530
body armor and six rows of fearsome teeth,
it’s little wonder why these predators are

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:08.830
so effective. Alien as this behavior may seem,
some sharks on earth employ a similar strategy

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:13.510
in swimming along the sandy floor near the
shoreline in search of food, although they

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:19.480
don’t possess the extreme tunneling abilities
of the Sand Shark. A common target of the

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:25.660
Sand Shark’s brutal ambushes swims overhead.
The Spadefish doesn’t look like much from

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:31.280
most angles, but glimpse one from above and
you’ll spot its defining feature: a single,

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:37.310
cyclopean eye. Closer analysis of the Spadefish
suggests it feeds on the sea floor, and its

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:43.460
single eye allows the spadefish to spot predators
above it. Its mottled green coloration suggests

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:49.960
it normally hides among sea grasses like many
fish do on earth. When a Spadefish wanders

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:57.940
onto the Grassy Plateaus, however, it is hopelessly
exposed… On the far side of the Grassy Plateau,

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:04.570
what appear to be tiny floating islands hang
miraculously in the water. Upon closer inspection,

00:10:04.570 --> 00:10:10.380
these chunks of rock are all suspended by
colonies of the same, curious lifeform: the

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:16.680
Floaters. Transparent, gelatinous blobs, floaters
are at once oddly beautiful, and a little

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:22.380
silly looking. Analysis of these creatures
reveal they’re actually multiple microorganisms

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:28.200
living in symbiosis, which we can compare
to some deep-sea species of jellyfish. The

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:34.560
floater’s ability to suspend such heavy
objects, however, remains something of a mystery.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:39.290
Swimming close by the floating islands is
a distant relative of the peeper called the

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:45.390
Reginald. The entire back end of these oddly-named
fellows is composed of one large caudal fin,

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:50.230
and the fin’s bright coloration appears
to be part of a mating ritual. Biologists

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:56.720
believe the Reginald filters algae and other
plant material from the water via four gill-like,

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:02.980
forward-facing orifices. While this is a noteworthy
adaptation, something looming behind the Reginald

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:10.130
demands our attention. In the abyss beyond
the edge of the plateau, the largest organism

00:11:10.130 --> 00:11:23.690
we’ve yet to glimpse calls out through the
deep. This is a Reefback: a leviathan-class

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:31.810
organism that exceeds lengths of 220 feet
or 70 meters, and is, thankfully, quite friendly.

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:36.520
Swimming closer to these gentle giants, we
can see they’re unlike any lifeform we’ve

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:44.400
yet to glimpse. Possessing a thick exoskeleton,
their shells have become an oasis of life.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:49.430
Adult Reefbacks can support mini-ecosystems
on their back: including many types of coral

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:55.260
and small fish. And nearby this adult is its
baby: a much smaller creature whose shell

00:11:55.260 --> 00:12:01.210
is not yet ready to support an ecosystem like
its parent’s. I confess I spent some time

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:06.440
just watching these creatures drift in their
pods, listening to them call out to each other

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:19.990
with complex sounds that suggest a surprising
amount of intelligence. With the sun now getting

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:25.570
low, it’s time to venture deeper into the
abyss, and enter the foreboding final biome

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:34.500
of the Dunes. In this fast, empty expanse,
life often flocks near hydrothermal vents.

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:39.520
These great fissures on the sea floor release
a rich chemical soup into the water, much

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:44.860
like those that support life in the depths
of Earth’s oceans. But it’s not life among

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:55.750
the vents that concerns me… A terrifying
roar comes from above. A Reaper Leviathan

00:12:55.750 --> 00:13:02.210
strikes out in the abyss. This aggressive
apex predator possesses four mandibles at

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:09.100
the front of its maw to drag in large prey.
And at 180 feet, or 55 meters in length, a

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:15.200
hungry Reaper Leviathan can take down almost
anything. Analysis of the organism’s biology

00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:20.150
suggests the deep roar emitted by the Reaper
is a form of sonar – meaning if you can

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:27.790
hear it, the reaper can see you. Thankfully,
it seems this Reaper isn’t hungry. And as

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:32.790
I watch the deadly leviathan vanish into the
darkness, I realize I’ve done enough field

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:42.010
work for the day… The sun has now set on
4546B. Yet the wonders of this planet continue,

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:47.610
as in the low light, various regions of the
sea are transformed by stunning displays of

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:54.620
bioluminescence. Even the Reefback pod from
earlier is now giving off a striking glow.

00:13:54.620 --> 00:14:03.300
This eerie beauty suggests the many hidden
wonders we have yet to see. To the north,

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:10.160
beyond the grassy plateaus, huge Tree Mushrooms
grow in abundance. Despite their name and

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:15.410
appearance, mushroom trees are actually a
particularly long-lived species of coral.

00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:21.300
In the shade of these ancient giants, a new
classification of life is swimming. These

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:28.080
are Jellyrays: an ethereal ray species with
highly translucent bodies. In Earth’s oceans,

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:33.320
translucent lifeforms are quite common in
the lower-light depths. The jellyrays are

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:42.300
a sign we’re getting closer to the deeper
— and more fantastical — regions of 4546B.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:47.260
Beyond the safety of the Mushroom Forest,
the twilight of the Bulb Zone awaits. Here,

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:53.360
the luminescent Bulb Bushes dominate the landscape,
and have evolved to grow in this rocky, sandy

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:59.040
environment thanks to a highly advanced system
of roots. And along the sea floor, raging

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:04.920
volcanic geysers launch boiling liquid and
chunks of molten rock into the surrounding

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:11.070
water. The extreme heat from these geysers
charbroils any fish that swims too close.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:19.130
An ominous light flickers in the murk. The
source is an ampeel: a huge, serpentine predator

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:25.140
covered with glowing prongs that produce zaps
of electricity. These aggressive creatures

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:30.690
use electric discharges to intimidate other
predators, and to kill their prey with a single

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:37.050
deadly shock. Such living batteries might
seem biologically inexplicable, but the curious

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:42.300
electric eel on earth might help demonstrate
how a creature could produce electricity.

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:48.910
Electric eels use disc-shaped cells packed
into specialized organs to generate their

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:54.690
electric currents — as incredible as that
might sound. It’s possible that the ampeel’s

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:59.980
prongs contain similar cells: although the
bio-electricity of the ampeel is far more

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:07.570
extreme. A series of screeches announces the
approach of a legion of Bonesharks. Highly

00:16:07.570 --> 00:16:14.010
aggressive and territorial carnivores, it’s
unlucky to encounter so many at once. Their

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:18.690
thickly-armored exoskeletons which give them
their names are adapted not just to deter

00:16:18.690 --> 00:16:25.590
larger predators, but also other Bonesharks.
The distinctive segmentation of these exoskeletons

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:29.780
suggests Bonesharks might share a distant
relative to the Sand Sharks we glimpsed on

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:37.650
our last outing. If so, it’s a fearsome
family tree. While returning to the shallows

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:43.001
to prepare for the next leg of my journey,
I spot a new type of colorful fish. Something

00:16:43.001 --> 00:16:48.382
about the way it looks is truly hypnotic,
and I want to swim closer, and closer… And

00:16:48.382 --> 00:16:54.900
then it bites my nose. That is a Mesmer, a
sneaky little creature that looks harmless

00:16:54.900 --> 00:17:00.220
while its four fins are folded up, but unfurled
can create hypnotic patterns that have evolved

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:05.470
to distract prey while it goes in for the
kill. Thankfully, it seems I’m a bit too

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:12.220
big to be swallowed up — but this is one
fish to avoid. With supplies replenished,

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:18.610
it’s time to venture further into the unknown.
Under the seafloor, networks of caves lead

00:17:18.610 --> 00:17:26.439
deeper into the uncharted interior of planet
4546B. Some of these caves are massive — and

00:17:26.439 --> 00:17:33.110
contain entire ecosystems of their own. And
the Jellyshroom Cave is one of the most miraculous.

00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:39.400
Here, among the stalagmites, grow Jellyshrooms:
massive, highly luminescent ¬flora which

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:45.400
might be distantly related to the tiny acid
mushrooms of the shallows. In the purple light

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:51.180
of the Jellyshrooms shine the Biters: small
carnivores that stalk the caves for small

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:57.120
prey. The antenna on their forehead seems
to resemble the deep-sea anglerfish’s lure

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:03.400
at first glance, but the Biter’s antenna
actually serves an entirely different purpose.

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:09.360
It contains olfactory organs that help it
detect bodily fluids in the water. In other

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:15.040
words, the Biter can smell its prey anywhere
in the cavern. Unless, of course, the Biter

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:22.050
becomes prey itself. Winding through the cave
is an unsettlingly lengthy Crabsnake: the

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:28.220
apex predator of this environment. What makes
this predator so deadly is not just its size,

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:33.660
but its ability to hide in plain sight. The
Crabsnake has a symbiotic relationship with

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:38.880
the local Jellyshrooms: using their hollow
interior as the perfect lair from which to

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:44.670
ambush prey. In behavior and appearance, the
Crabsnake somewhat resembles Earth’s Bobbit

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:51.630
Worm: an unattractive predator that also ambushes
its prey. In the Jellyshroom caves, one needs

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:59.360
to keep an eye out… Elsewhere on the seafloor,
another shadowy cavern beckons. After many

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:04.353
hours navigating the cave system, I surface
in a cave that, strangely, seems to be lit

00:19:04.353 --> 00:19:10.430
by… daylight? I realize I’ve stumbled
upon something exceedingly rare for planet

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:18.640
4546B: dry land. This tiny island is the peak
of an undersea mountain connected to an aquatic

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:23.720
cave system, and it’s one of the few places
on the planet you can encounter terrestrial

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:31.100
life. Scuttling on the beach are Cave Crawlers.
As their name implies, these crab-like creatures

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:36.240
have evolved to crawl along the sides of cave
walls: which is likely why they are able to

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:42.100
adapt to this fleeting landmass relatively
quickly. Analysis of their biology suggests

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:47.460
they possess a gas exchange membrane to breathe
air, instead of fully-functional terrestrial

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:55.130
lungs. Soaring overhead is a lifeform that
has spent much more time adapting to life.

00:19:55.130 --> 00:20:02.191
This is a Skyray: an avian creature that has,
remarkably, evolved powered flight. Although

00:20:02.191 --> 00:20:08.510
its wings give it some resemblance to aquatic
ray species, analysis shows no particular

00:20:08.510 --> 00:20:13.990
genetic link. Instead, the Skyray appears
to have split off the evolutionary tree much

00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:20.030
earlier than most other species on the planet,
perhaps at a time when dry land was more prevalent…

00:20:20.030 --> 00:20:29.260
In any case, it seems we’ve now seen all
the island has to offer. Or… maybe not.

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:35.260
On one end of the island is a tremendous artificial
spire. This mysterious monolith appears to

00:20:35.260 --> 00:20:40.340
be the work of advanced extraterrestrials
who, much like myself, came to this planet

00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:47.790
from afar. But did they come to study the
life here, or were their intentions more sinister…?

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:54.590
In any case, it seems this planet’s greatest
secret is still waiting to be uncovered…

00:20:54.590 --> 00:21:01.690
To the south, the seafloor plunges lower than
ever before. We have come upon the Grand Reef.

00:21:01.690 --> 00:21:07.100
Here, orb-like blue plants called anchor pods
risk floating off due to their gas-filled

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:13.810
membrane — only staying in place due to
their net-like root system. And nearby stomp

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:20.350
a herd of Sea Treader Leviathans. These vast
herbivores roam the depths in search of plants

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:26.559
on which to graze. The thunderous nature of
their rhythmic steps is truly something to

00:21:26.559 --> 00:21:38.910
experience. Perhaps the most curious thing
about these creatures is the fact they have

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:44.980
three legs — something almost never glimpsed
in nature. Analysis suggests their front leg

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:50.770
is actually an elongated snout, used to siphon
plant material from the sea floor, and to

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:56.510
maintain balance. As alien as this might seem,
there are some species of Earth Tripod fish

00:21:56.510 --> 00:22:03.220
with elongated fins that they use similarly
to stand on the sea floor. Right now, it appears

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:12.930
the Sea Treaders are migrating, so it’s
best not to disturb them… Beneath the Grand

00:22:12.930 --> 00:22:19.010
Reef, caverns take us further into the abyss.
Here, the intimidating Crabsquid waits to

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:25.090
butcher prey with its ten limbs. Swim close
to one, and they’ll unleash a mysterious

00:22:25.090 --> 00:22:31.371
bio-electric discharge: which has the properties
of an electro-magnetic pulse. While they appear

00:22:31.371 --> 00:22:36.550
to have massive brains, analysis suggests
the large organ within their translucent sac

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:42.620
is actually their stomach, which the Crabsquid
fill with their bio-luminescent prey. As a

00:22:42.620 --> 00:22:47.720
result, they seem attracted to light sources,
convincing me to make a hasty exit from my

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:54.090
submarine. This turns out to be a good call,
as it drags my submarine into the depths.

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:58.880
If there are other creatures like the Crabsquid
on the prowl, I’m going to need something

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:06.040
a little larger... For the next perilous leg
of the journey, I’ll be piloting a ‘Cyclops’

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:11.730
submarine: a vehicle that should be able to
withstand the crushing depth of the abyss.

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:18.420
Below the deep Grand Reef awaits a surreal
environment of eerie green fog. This is the

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:25.200
Lost River, a region covered in alkaline brine
pools, which give the illusion of an underwater

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:30.530
stream. Brine pools also exist in Earth’s
oceans, and are extremely toxic due to their

00:23:30.530 --> 00:23:37.430
high salinity. Best not to get too close…
Above these pools drift the haunting Ghost

00:23:37.430 --> 00:23:42.950
Rays. Relatives of the Jellyrays from the
Mushroom Forest, in these extreme depths,

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:47.380
the Ghost Rays have become almost completely
transparent, to the point where you can see

00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:53.000
the red of their internal organs. Elsewhere
in the Lost River, Blood Crawlers — the

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:59.721
spindly relatives of the Cave Crawlers — stride
above the brine on stilt-like legs. Rather

00:23:59.721 --> 00:24:07.250
unsettling forms of life… In the central
chamber, something truly horrifying is waiting.

00:24:07.250 --> 00:24:09.920
Or rather, it would be if this leviathan were
still alive. This ancient fossil belonged

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:16.900
to a colossus far larger than any that currently
swims through the seas. Indeed, it is so massive

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:22.250
that its ribs have become part of the Lost
River’s landscape. We should count ourselves

00:24:22.250 --> 00:24:29.550
lucky this unknown genus seems to have gone
extinct long ago… Yet still alive are the

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:35.840
River Prowlers. These aggressive, transparent
predators haunt the gravesite of the leviathan

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:42.250
like vengeful ghosts. Even more frightening,
their four head-tendrils can lash out when

00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:47.800
attacking, allowing them to pull in prey.
Not the sort of creature you’d like to encounter

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:54.980
in the dark. But there is a strange beauty
to certain regions of the Lost River. In a

00:24:54.980 --> 00:25:01.020
cove under the central chamber, the brine
becomes a harmless blue, and Ghost Rays flock

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:07.360
around a massive tree. This tree appears to
be an ancient nesting ground, as it has grown

00:25:07.360 --> 00:25:13.270
around a giant bio-luminescent egg. While
the egg is beautiful, whatever hatches out

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:20.630
of an egg that size might not be… To find
the adult form of the huge creature that hatches

00:25:20.630 --> 00:25:27.620
from this egg, we must venture into the nightmarish
darkness of the Dead Zone. Here, at the edge

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:32.900
of the habitable region for most fauna lives,
we’ve found our giants… or they’ve found

00:25:32.900 --> 00:25:40.980
us. These aggressive monsters are Ghost Leviathans,
and they’re a major threat to this expedition.

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:45.320
Analysis of their jaws suggest they feed on
microscopic lifeforms that help them reach

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:50.230
their massive sizes — although they also
seem fully capable of feeding on something

00:25:50.230 --> 00:25:57.270
larger. Time to head back the way we came…
The mystery of the giant egg has been solved,

00:25:57.270 --> 00:26:02.540
but that’s not the only strange thing in
the Lost River. These ruins of a massive alien

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:08.350
structure resemble the one on the island.
The structure is guarded by these lifeforms,

00:26:08.350 --> 00:26:15.210
the Warpers — who possess a mind-bending
ability. At will, they can somehow fold spacetime

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:21.370
and vanish into portals. This is not the ability
of a natural lifeform —it seems we are dealing

00:26:21.370 --> 00:26:28.360
with an artificial creation. Searching for
answers, I enter the ruined building. In here,

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:32.330
we can see the unfinished artificial body
of a Warper — confirming that they did not

00:26:32.330 --> 00:26:39.500
evolve naturally, and are a creation of extraterrestrials.
But for what purpose? Translating a digital

00:26:39.500 --> 00:26:45.840
message, it seems these alien architects came
to 4546 B many years ago searching for a cure

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:52.420
for a disease, and the Warpers are their artificial
quarantine enforcers. Given the lack of any

00:26:52.420 --> 00:26:59.980
living architects on 4546 B, it appears that
they were unsuccessful…On a table in a nearby

00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:05.530
lab, they’ve left behind the egg of a curious
creature. After returning to the surface to

00:27:05.530 --> 00:27:12.420
recharge my submarine, I hatch the egg and
this delightful lifeform emerges. This is

00:27:12.420 --> 00:27:17.970
a Cuddlefish: a friendly, rotund little creature
that displays high levels of intelligence

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:24.740
and curiosity. I’ve also found it’s quite
a fan of treats. Analysis suggests it’s

00:27:24.740 --> 00:27:29.330
possible this creature has been imported from
another world — or even genetically altered

00:27:29.330 --> 00:27:35.020
to be extra friendly. It’s possible the
architect aliens kept them as pets just as

00:27:35.020 --> 00:27:41.840
we do with dogs. It’s tempting to stay with
the Cuddlefish in the shallows, yet I can’t

00:27:41.840 --> 00:27:47.400
help but feel that in the abyss, there are
more mysteries I’ve yet to solve… Beneath

00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:53.590
the Lost River, the landscape blazes with
the fiery glow of molten rock. We’ve reached

00:27:53.590 --> 00:27:59.620
the Lava Zone: the deepest environment of
them all. Here, incredible, heat resistant

00:27:59.620 --> 00:28:05.610
forms of life have evolved: like the Crimson
Ray — a ray species that has developed a

00:28:05.610 --> 00:28:10.850
mottled coloring to help it blend in with
the lava. And the Magmarang: a relative of

00:28:10.850 --> 00:28:17.480
the Boomerang with a beautiful glowing tailfin.
Another remarkable form of life is the Lava

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:23.480
Larva, a grub-like creature that feeds off
geothermal and electrical energy. I first

00:28:23.480 --> 00:28:27.799
learned this fact when I found one stuck to
the windshield of my cyclops, draining its

00:28:27.799 --> 00:28:33.360
energy. While this behavior is somewhat annoying,
the energy-conversion capabilities of this

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:41.500
creature are truly remarkable. On the molten
floor is something highly alarming. These

00:28:41.500 --> 00:28:47.430
skeletons belong to Reaper Leviathans. It
seems that somewhere in the Lava Zone is a

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:55.490
predator so large it can drag Reaper Leviathans
into the depths. This doesn’t bode well...

00:28:55.490 --> 00:29:01.070
Basking in the red glow of the molten rock
are Lava Lizards. These predators are too

00:29:01.070 --> 00:29:06.630
small to be our culprits, but they’re nonetheless
quite deadly. For defense, they are able to

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:12.480
cover their bodies with hardened lava like
natural armor. While such a behavior is far

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:17.090
more extreme than anything on Earth, Scaly
Foot Snails — which I’ve discussed on

00:29:17.090 --> 00:29:22.370
this channel before — can survive in volcanic
vents by incorporating iron scales into their

00:29:22.370 --> 00:29:29.700
body like medieval armor. However, Lava Lizard
biology takes this to a new level. At the

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:34.710
end of the Lava Zone lurks a massive chamber.
The loudest roar we’ve encountered on our

00:29:34.710 --> 00:29:47.330
voyage shakes the cavern. We’ve found our
Reaper Leviathan slayer. This is a Sea Dragon

00:29:47.330 --> 00:29:56.100
Leviathan. Possessing both arms and tentacles,
these creatures exceed 350 feet, or 112 meters

00:29:56.100 --> 00:30:01.330
in length. Sea Dragons get their name from
their ability to consume molten materials

00:30:01.330 --> 00:30:07.470
and expel them at their adversaries like they’re
breathing fire. I can only hope this absurdly

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:14.790
deadly lifeform doesn’t notice me... no
such luck. A huge blow from the leviathan

00:30:14.790 --> 00:30:21.420
rocks the cyclops submarine. As a result,
there appears to be a… ‘minor’ leak.

00:30:21.420 --> 00:30:27.890
Out of options, I have to abandon ship. Luckily,
I have one last hope in the Prawn Suit: a

00:30:27.890 --> 00:30:33.309
deep sea mechanical walker that should make
an ideal escape pod. Ejecting from the cyclops,

00:30:33.309 --> 00:30:41.500
it’s a bit of a drop to the bottom. But
it appears my plan has worked, as the Sea

00:30:41.500 --> 00:30:48.320
Dragon Leviathan thinks its foe has been vanquished.
Here, at the end of our journey, lies a final

00:30:48.320 --> 00:30:55.370
mysterious building. With some difficultly,
I pilot the Prawn Suit over to it. Inside,

00:30:55.370 --> 00:31:02.790
locked in a containment facility, the largest
creature in Subnautica is waiting. This is

00:31:02.790 --> 00:31:08.860
a Sea Emperor Leviathan, and although it appears
to be a relative of the Sea Dragon, it is

00:31:08.860 --> 00:31:15.200
in no way hostile. Like real-world baleen
whales, the largest creatures on earth, its

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:21.131
diet consists solely of microorganisms. While
the Sea Emperor Leviathan is so large that

00:31:21.131 --> 00:31:26.570
I am unable to get a proper scan of it, it
seems that it can manipulate neurons to communicate

00:31:26.570 --> 00:31:32.280
telepathically — as it told me of its plight.
The Sea Emperor was entombed here by the alien

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:36.830
architects in the hopes it could provide a
cure for their disease — which I believe

00:31:36.830 --> 00:31:43.110
is in strict violation of the intergalactic
code of biological ethics. At the request

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:49.410
of this intelligent creature, I agree to take
its hatchlings back to the surface with me.

00:31:49.410 --> 00:31:55.381
Watching the first moments of baby Sea Emperors’
lives is a truly wonderous experience: even

00:31:55.381 --> 00:32:05.510
after all the biological marvels I’ve glimpsed
thus far. Sadly, the Sea Emperor is at the

00:32:05.510 --> 00:32:11.070
end of its lifespan, and informs me it won’t
be coming with us. At the very least, I’m

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:16.970
happy I can help their babies live on in freedom.
It is a bittersweet conclusion to a long and

00:32:16.970 --> 00:32:24.430
winding journey… With the Sea Emperor babies
returning to their natural habitat, our study

00:32:24.430 --> 00:32:34.549
of this region of Subnautica is at its end.
But there is another region of Planet 4546B.

00:32:34.549 --> 00:32:40.940
Sector Zero is a region of extremely low temperatures,
where organisms of all types have had to adapt

00:32:40.940 --> 00:32:46.400
to the cold. Beginning on the ice floats above
the shallows, a curious lifeform makes its

00:32:46.400 --> 00:32:51.920
home in this region where land meets the sea.
Huddled together on a float is a flock of

00:32:51.920 --> 00:32:57.950
waddling Pengwings. Pengwings are small, predatory
fauna which… aren’t the most graceful

00:32:57.950 --> 00:33:03.510
on land. When a Pengwing dives underwater,
however, the creature’s unique retractable

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:10.380
tail allows them to twist through the currents
with remarkable grace. Despite their appearance,

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:15.370
Pengwings are carnivores, and their beak opens
horizontally, revealing a rather alarming

00:33:15.370 --> 00:33:21.300
set of teeth. In their body shape and behavior,
Pengwings show a high degree of convergence

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:26.970
with Earth penguins — with both species
adapting to an arctic environment in similar

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:33.100
ways. And on any ice-float with Pengwings,
you will also find Penglings — the juvenile

00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:38.419
form of Pengwings who never stray far from
their parents. Even more than adults, infant

00:33:38.419 --> 00:33:44.360
Penglings are extremely awkward on land. But
what they might lack in overall balance, they

00:33:44.360 --> 00:33:49.870
make up for in endless enthusiasm. And while
Penglings might possess the same, somewhat

00:33:49.870 --> 00:33:55.400
alarming fanged mouth, they’re remarkably
docile creatures, perfectly content with being

00:33:55.400 --> 00:34:02.930
gently lifted up. As nice as it is to briefly
hold a baby Pengling, however, I don’t want

00:34:02.930 --> 00:34:10.040
to disrupt their natural routines… As we
dive into the polar waters, a shallow, surprisingly

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:16.279
warm region awaits just under the ice floats.
The landscape of this coastal sea is dominated

00:34:16.279 --> 00:34:22.049
by Coral Bridges, great arches formed from
rapidly growing coral polyps that can exceed

00:34:22.049 --> 00:34:29.379
600 feet, or 200 meters in length. Bioluminescent
blue barnacles grow on the Coral’s underside,

00:34:29.379 --> 00:34:34.129
which a scan suggests are actually a separate
species taking advantage of the bridges’

00:34:34.129 --> 00:34:39.970
structure. But why would the coral bridges
grow into such strange, arching shapes? The

00:34:39.970 --> 00:34:45.229
answer lies in a phenomenon known as Thigmotropism
— a pattern of growth observed in Earth

00:34:45.229 --> 00:34:51.810
plants in response to touch. Thigmotropism
is what allows vines to wrap around and ‘climb’

00:34:51.810 --> 00:34:57.369
up poles, and it’s the likely reason the
great Coral bridges always grow from one rock

00:34:57.369 --> 00:35:03.869
to another… Just beyond these coral bridges,
we encounter an Arctic Peeper, who watches

00:35:03.869 --> 00:35:09.559
for predators with its massive yellow eyes.
Relatives of the Common Peeper in the tropical

00:35:09.559 --> 00:35:14.410
seas, Arctic Peepers’ scales have evolved
a whitish hue for better camouflage against

00:35:14.410 --> 00:35:20.339
the ice. Other familiar herbivores also swim
here, like the translucent Bladderfish and

00:35:20.339 --> 00:35:26.040
the vibrant hoopfish. Together, these species
form an important base in the Sector Zero

00:35:26.040 --> 00:35:34.160
food chain for the larger, less familiar creatures
that lurk under the ice. Swimming northwards,

00:35:34.160 --> 00:35:40.210
I’ve reached a region known as the Sparse
Arctic. This entire biome is situated under

00:35:40.210 --> 00:35:46.249
thick ice shelves, and receives little direct
sunlight. On the underside of these icebergs,

00:35:46.249 --> 00:35:52.550
hazardous ice formations known as Brinicles
protrude like jagged fangs. Highly unstable,

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:58.609
Brinicles frequently shatter and crash to
the sea floor. Yet as foreboding as the Sparse

00:35:58.609 --> 00:36:05.090
Arctic seems, some truly remarkable species
thrive here… This odd-looking fellow is

00:36:05.090 --> 00:36:11.420
called a Brinewing. With its rotund form and
beak-like mouth, it gives the impression of

00:36:11.420 --> 00:36:17.359
an ineffectual herbivore. But the Brinewing
is a powerful predator. To hunt, it unleashes

00:36:17.359 --> 00:36:23.249
a rapid-fire torrent of a supercooled saline
solution that freezes in the water, and can

00:36:23.249 --> 00:36:29.329
encase its prey in ice. A scan suggests the
Brinewing uses a highly-specialized bladder

00:36:29.329 --> 00:36:35.359
to produce this supercooled brine from the
surrounding salt water. As unusal an adaptation

00:36:35.359 --> 00:36:40.890
as this might seem, the Earth Archerfish can
shoot down insects using water jets launched

00:36:40.890 --> 00:36:47.650
from their specialized mouths… and are remarkably
accurate. Other species in the Sparse Arctic

00:36:47.650 --> 00:36:53.880
have very different survival strategies. Like
the enormous Titan Holefish — a slow moving

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:59.690
herbivore with a strange hole in the center
of its body. The Titan Holefish is a relative

00:36:59.690 --> 00:37:05.279
of the tiny, guppy-sized tropical holefish
— and is so much larger than its cousin

00:37:05.279 --> 00:37:10.510
you can actually swim through the hole at
its center. But what is the purpose of this

00:37:10.510 --> 00:37:16.739
hole? A scan suggests the gel-like interior
is lined with hundreds of eggs, but curiously,

00:37:16.739 --> 00:37:23.089
their DNA doesn’t match that of the Holefish…
The mystery of the eggs is revealed when I

00:37:23.089 --> 00:37:29.810
spot a tiny lifeform swimming alongside the
Holefish. Soon, many others join in, swarming

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:36.359
like a bee colony around a hive. These are
Symbiotes — small predators that have evolved

00:37:36.359 --> 00:37:41.999
to make their nests inside the hole of the
Titan Holefish, and in turn defend them at

00:37:41.999 --> 00:37:48.089
all costs — and even provide them with food.
This highly cooperative lifestyle is an example

00:37:48.089 --> 00:37:54.930
of a mutualistic partnership — one of the
few on Planet 4546 B. As a result, the Titan

00:37:54.930 --> 00:38:01.390
Holefish has evolved to be remarkably unintelligent:
swimming forward on impulse, fully trusting

00:38:01.390 --> 00:38:09.239
the complex ecosystem that supports its every
need. Just beyond the Sparse Arctic, the bare

00:38:09.239 --> 00:38:15.859
sand gives way to the teeming Arctic Kelp
Forest. Here, Creepvine Plants sway in the

00:38:15.859 --> 00:38:22.519
polar current, casting light into the depths
with their bioluminescent seeds. These Creepvine

00:38:22.519 --> 00:38:27.970
plants are close relatives of the green variety
which grow in the warm waters of the tropics,

00:38:27.970 --> 00:38:33.130
although the chemical composition of the polar
waters has turned them a pale yellow. Among

00:38:33.130 --> 00:38:38.720
the fronds, familiar fish like the boomerang
flit between the leaves... …And some very

00:38:38.720 --> 00:38:45.940
unfamiliar fish swim here also. These extremely
bizarre lifeforms are called Spinner Fish,

00:38:45.940 --> 00:38:51.839
and have perhaps the most peculiar method
of swimming ever conceived. Using three pairs

00:38:51.839 --> 00:38:58.000
of siphons on the side of their body, Spinner
Fish jet along like living waterwheels. At

00:38:58.000 --> 00:39:04.250
a glance, its bizarre body the plan somewhat
resembles the larval form of the ocean sunfish.

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:10.359
But ultimately, the Spinner Fish is one of
a kind. Speaking of one of a kind, I soon

00:39:10.359 --> 00:39:16.049
spot an incredible lifeform. I’m just about
to scan it when it swims in and snatches the

00:39:16.049 --> 00:39:22.700
scanner from my hands. Frustrated, I give
chase, and find the creature looks back and

00:39:22.700 --> 00:39:28.710
waves the stolen scanner to mock me. Thankfully
I’m able to cut it off and snatch my tool

00:39:28.710 --> 00:39:33.930
back. Finishing the scan, I discover this
creature might be annoying, but it’s truly

00:39:33.930 --> 00:39:39.700
remarkable, with both a developed brain and
finger like appendages for object manipulation.

00:39:39.700 --> 00:39:45.220
It’s called a Sea Monkey — a nickname
it shares with Earth brine shrimp. Unlike

00:39:45.220 --> 00:39:51.200
those Sea Monkeys, however, the Sea Monkey
of Planet 4546 B seems about as intelligent

00:39:51.200 --> 00:39:56.569
as an actual primate — meaning it’s the
smartest lifeform I’ve found in sector zero.

00:39:56.569 --> 00:40:02.510
But given time, the Sea Monkeys might have
an incredible future trajectory. I just hope

00:40:02.510 --> 00:40:08.170
they evolve to be less annoying… With the
Kelp Forest thoroughly charted, it’s time

00:40:08.170 --> 00:40:13.479
to regroup on the surface. Sticking my head
above the water, however, I find the shallows

00:40:13.479 --> 00:40:19.319
are engulfed in a dangerous polar storm. Looking
around, I realize that the Pengwing flock

00:40:19.319 --> 00:40:25.359
from earlier might be in serious danger. Upon
arriving at their ice float, however, I find

00:40:25.359 --> 00:40:31.170
the Pengwings unbothered by the weather, simply
huddling for warmth and waiting out the rain.

00:40:31.170 --> 00:40:36.280
Truly, Pengwings are intriguing creatures.
With the sun thankfully returning, it occurs

00:40:36.280 --> 00:40:40.859
to me that there’s much I still have to
learn about Pengwing flock dynamics, but I

00:40:40.859 --> 00:40:47.119
don’t want to disrupt them with my presence.
Time for some innovation. I’ve devised an

00:40:47.119 --> 00:40:53.940
alternative, less conspicuous method of observation.
This machine is my Spy Pengling, and it will

00:40:53.940 --> 00:40:59.180
hopefully be able to keep an eye on the Pengwing
flock with its hidden cameras while I continue

00:40:59.180 --> 00:41:05.200
my expedition across Sector Zero. I’m uncertain
if the Pengwings will be fooled into accepting

00:41:05.200 --> 00:41:11.650
this robot as one of their own… but the
only way to find out is to test it. As inconspicuously

00:41:11.650 --> 00:41:18.339
as I can, I deploy the robot. Taking control
via an axillary remote, I navigate the Spy

00:41:18.339 --> 00:41:24.170
Pengling into the midst of the flock. It’s…
not a perfect disguise. But the Pengwings

00:41:24.170 --> 00:41:29.339
don’t seem particularly bothered by its
presence. Though it’s unlikely this robot

00:41:29.339 --> 00:41:34.380
is fooling them, it doesn’t appear to disrupt
their natural behavior. I’ll leave the flock

00:41:34.380 --> 00:41:40.640
under its watchful eye, and head deeper into
the unknown regions... Even further out to

00:41:40.640 --> 00:41:47.109
sea, the mysterious Lilypad Islands hang in
the water, seemingly by miraculous means.

00:41:47.109 --> 00:41:52.690
In truth, these floating chunks of rock are
held up by Giant Lilypads: an enormous type

00:41:52.690 --> 00:41:59.249
of plant that define this ecosystem. Giant
Lilypads are indeed so large that it’s possible

00:41:59.249 --> 00:42:03.750
to stand on their leafy tops without fear
of sinking. But it’s the bizarre life that

00:42:03.750 --> 00:42:10.539
swims in the lilypads’ shadows that make
this region truly unique… Take, for example,

00:42:10.539 --> 00:42:16.599
the hilariously named Noot Fish. With its
colorful markings and innocent, beak-like

00:42:16.599 --> 00:42:22.160
snout, the Noot fish can easily pass as a
herbivore. But any fish that makes that mistake

00:42:22.160 --> 00:42:28.729
is soon swallowed whole. The Noot Fish’s
beak is in fact a stretchy, extendable lip,

00:42:28.729 --> 00:42:34.960
lined with barb-like teeth it uses to envelop
prey. This kind of extendible mouth somewhat

00:42:34.960 --> 00:42:40.220
resembles that of the Sarcastic Fringehead
of the Pacific Ocean. Best to keep an eye

00:42:40.220 --> 00:42:46.700
out. But there are bigger predators here than
the Noot Fish. The Brute Shark is an aggressive

00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:53.650
predator that scours the depths with its ghostly
white eyes. With huge fangs and a massive,

00:42:53.650 --> 00:42:59.769
bony jaw, they feed on just about anything
they catch. Like many Earth sharks, they prefer

00:42:59.769 --> 00:43:06.009
to attack via ambush from above or below.
The only good news is that while they’re

00:43:06.009 --> 00:43:11.269
deadly, Brute Sharks aren’t particularly
fast. Still, if I don’t keep my distance,

00:43:11.269 --> 00:43:18.440
I might be on the menu... Closer to the surface,
I spot a remarkably colorful lifeform. Swimming

00:43:18.440 --> 00:43:26.529
nearer, the edges of my vision blur, and I
feel just a little dizzzzy. Huh. Has the sky

00:43:26.529 --> 00:43:35.140
always been that color? Everything feels…
floaty for a while. Eventually, the effect

00:43:35.140 --> 00:43:40.979
wears off, and with a clear head, I understand
what just happened. That lifeform, known as

00:43:40.979 --> 00:43:46.700
the Lily Paddler, possesses the unique ability
to disorient would-be attackers with the mesmerizing

00:43:46.700 --> 00:43:52.650
patterns on its colorful appendages. It's
an impressive defense, and a vital one for

00:43:52.650 --> 00:43:57.859
a creature like a Lily Paddler which is otherwise
quite vulnerable. I just wish it didn’t

00:43:57.859 --> 00:44:05.489
make my head hurt. A shadow passes overhead.
Just above me, a whale-sized leviathan opens

00:44:05.489 --> 00:44:12.549
its mouth to a tremendous size and swallows
an entire school of fish in a single gulp.

00:44:12.549 --> 00:44:17.969
This is a Glow Whale, and while they’re
highly effective at feeding on small fish,

00:44:17.969 --> 00:44:23.940
they’re thankfully quite gentle. At almost
100 feet, or 30 meters in length, they’re

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:29.000
the first Leviathan class organism I’ve
encountered in Sector Zero — and a highly

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:34.369
intelligent one. A scan of the Glow Whales
indicate they possess many organs that are

00:44:34.369 --> 00:44:40.469
capable of bioluminescence, most notably around
the corners of their eyes and the ends of

00:44:40.469 --> 00:44:46.140
their horns. Careful not to startle it, I
brush a Glow Whale under its eye, causing

00:44:46.140 --> 00:44:52.140
a faint glow. It’s difficult to interpret
whether this is a random response, or an attempt

00:44:52.140 --> 00:44:58.269
to send a message. In any case, the Glow Whale
is content with my presence. As I watch it

00:44:58.269 --> 00:45:04.430
rejoin its pod, I wish I could stay with this
specular lifeform longer. But before the sun

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:11.650
sets, there’s one last region I need to
chart… Underneath layers of snow and ice,

00:45:11.650 --> 00:45:17.309
there are telltale signs that a permanent
land mass makes up a portion of Sector Zero.

00:45:17.309 --> 00:45:22.130
Dry land is so scarce on this ocean planet
that it’s impossible to guess what sorts

00:45:22.130 --> 00:45:26.279
of lifeforms might live further inland. We’ll
have to see for ourselves. Passing through

00:45:26.279 --> 00:45:33.839
a narrow canyon, I find strange plant-animal
hybrids that seem to be growing from the walls.

00:45:33.839 --> 00:45:39.980
These are Thermal Lilies, organisms that spend
most of the time tracking the Sun and absorbing

00:45:39.980 --> 00:45:46.950
its energy, but also turn to track my movement
as I pass. A scan suggests they aren’t dangerous,

00:45:46.950 --> 00:45:51.079
and thanks to the thermal radiation they’ve
absorbed, they’re actually a welcome source

00:45:51.079 --> 00:45:57.599
of heat in Sector Zero’s frigid interior.
Still, it’s unsettling the way they follow

00:45:57.599 --> 00:46:05.239
me… Beyond the canyon, a snowy landscape
far too vast to traverse on foot awaits. Luckily,

00:46:05.239 --> 00:46:12.140
I have an alternate method of transportation.
This is a Snowfox, a hoverbike designed for

00:46:12.140 --> 00:46:18.989
land-based expeditions. Zooming over the arctic
terrain, I find it’s got a bit of a kick.

00:46:18.989 --> 00:46:24.660
At these speeds, I should be able to chart
this region fairly effectively. A short time

00:46:24.660 --> 00:46:33.480
later, I spot a hairy creature in the distance.
The question is, is it friendly? …Not friendly.

00:46:33.480 --> 00:46:39.150
Putting the Snowfox in reverse, I discover
the creature gives chase with alarming speed.

00:46:39.150 --> 00:46:44.729
After several near misses, I back away to
a safe distance. That quadrupedal predator

00:46:44.729 --> 00:46:51.489
was a Snow Stalker, and while it nearly sank
its claws into me, I can’t help but be excited

00:46:51.489 --> 00:46:58.660
at the discovery of a terrestrial land animal
on Planet 4546B! With its white fur and imposing

00:46:58.660 --> 00:47:04.769
bulk, the Snow Stalker has converged on a
body plan similar to Earth polar bears. I’m

00:47:04.769 --> 00:47:11.229
curious about the lineage of such a lifeform.
A brief analysis reveals the Snow Stalker

00:47:11.229 --> 00:47:17.160
is actually a distant relative of the fully
aquatic Stalker I encountered in the tropical

00:47:17.160 --> 00:47:22.289
region. While these two species diverged a
long time ago, there are still some telltale

00:47:22.289 --> 00:47:28.151
signs of their relation in the shape of their
head and their dorsal spines. A fascinating

00:47:28.151 --> 00:47:34.359
genetic ancestry. Speaking of which, a short
way off from the Snow Stalker, I spot what

00:47:34.359 --> 00:47:40.410
appears to be its cub playing in the snow.
Just a fraction of the size of its parent,

00:47:40.410 --> 00:47:47.279
this baby Snow Stalker is far less aggressive
than its parent. Well, mostly… Trekking

00:47:47.279 --> 00:47:53.979
onward, I reach an icesheet marked by jagged,
towering ice formations. This region is known

00:47:53.979 --> 00:48:00.499
as the Arctic Spires, and it’s home to a
giant… A huge head bursts from below the

00:48:00.499 --> 00:48:07.000
ice. Seconds later, a massive, serpentine
leviathan rises into the air, and nearly takes

00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:12.589
me out with a swipe from its massive horn.
This terror of the frozen plains is an Ice

00:48:12.589 --> 00:48:20.950
Worm. At over three hundred feet, or 90 meters,
it’s almost twice as long as a Reaper Leviathan,

00:48:20.950 --> 00:48:27.289
and by far the largest creature I’ve seen
in Sector Zero. Its glowing horn is superheated

00:48:27.289 --> 00:48:33.349
through an exothermic chemical reaction — allowing
it to melt the very ice its prey is standing

00:48:33.349 --> 00:48:39.839
on. Time to make a fast exit. Speeding away
on my Snowfox, I spot the Snowstalker and

00:48:39.839 --> 00:48:45.759
its cub from earlier fleeing beside me. We
barely make it to safety. With creatures like

00:48:45.759 --> 00:48:52.140
the Ice Worm around, I think I might put off
doing more field work till tomorrow. While

00:48:52.140 --> 00:48:58.349
the sun sets overhead, I check in to find
my Spy Pengling has become a veritable member

00:48:58.349 --> 00:49:04.269
of the flock, and has done an excellent job
keeping an eye on its feathered companions.

00:49:04.269 --> 00:49:11.430
Together, my pengling bot and I sit and watch
the Sector Zero sunset. And with the night

00:49:11.430 --> 00:49:18.269
comes a final surprise. The sky is lit up
by a dazzling Aurora similar to the Aurora

00:49:18.269 --> 00:49:23.539
Borealis in the northern latitudes of earth.
On our planet, these ‘northern lights’

00:49:23.539 --> 00:49:29.479
are a result of disturbances in the magnetosphere
caused by solar wind — and we can theorize

00:49:29.479 --> 00:49:36.180
they have the same origin on planet 4546B.
It’s a beautiful, imposing display that

00:49:36.180 --> 00:49:41.900
can make one feel a little isolated in the
universe. But with my Spy Pengling beside

00:49:41.900 --> 00:49:48.710
me, I don’t feel truly alone. A transmission
interrupts my thoughts. It seems I’m picking

00:49:48.710 --> 00:49:57.829
up a mysterious communication… but from
where? And who, or what, is sending it out?

00:49:57.829 --> 00:50:03.180
The mysterious transmission is emanating from
the murky waters of a region known as the

00:50:03.180 --> 00:50:09.779
Deep Twisty Bridges. Diving into these alien
depths, the only light comes from the bioluminescent

00:50:09.779 --> 00:50:15.710
coral bridges that spiral throughout this
cavernous abyss. The dimness of the deep has

00:50:15.710 --> 00:50:23.430
transformed all life that swims in the gloom.
Like pale ghosts, the skeletal Spinefish haunt

00:50:23.430 --> 00:50:29.589
the depths. A subspecies of the hoopfish,
the Spinefish have become nearly translucent,

00:50:29.589 --> 00:50:35.349
and possess rib-like markings that give them
a deathly appearance. The phenomenon of certain

00:50:35.349 --> 00:50:40.670
deep-sea fish becoming transparent is present
in Earth’s oceans as well — notably in

00:50:40.670 --> 00:50:46.029
the Barreleye Fish — an animal that possess
a head made of translucent tissue to allow

00:50:46.029 --> 00:50:52.920
it to look upwards and spot its preferred
prey. Truthfully, the Barreleye Fish is an

00:50:52.920 --> 00:50:59.059
animal that would seem right at home in the
oceans of Planet 4546B. But the Spinefish

00:50:59.059 --> 00:51:04.680
isn’t a carnivore, so despite its haunting
appearance, it’s no real danger. But there

00:51:04.680 --> 00:51:11.709
are genuine dangers here. A huge predator
swims, torpedo-like, among the bridges. With

00:51:11.709 --> 00:51:17.430
unexpected speed, it rushes in and attempts
to swallow me whole, a fate I’m barely able

00:51:17.430 --> 00:51:23.819
to avoid by prying its jaws open. The predator
continues to give chase, but I escape by swimming

00:51:23.819 --> 00:51:30.739
into a crevasse it can’t reach. That terror
of the abyss was a Squidshark — a highly

00:51:30.739 --> 00:51:36.329
aggressive fauna species that is the apex
predator of this region. Analysis suggests

00:51:36.329 --> 00:51:42.140
its terrifying burst of speed comes from two
vents on either side of its body, which provide

00:51:42.140 --> 00:51:48.359
jet propulsion. Through this adaptation, the
Squidshark, like its name suggests, combines

00:51:48.359 --> 00:51:54.400
the most formidable aspects of deep-sea squids
and macropredator shark species. The result

00:51:54.400 --> 00:51:59.890
is a deadly amalgamation I’ll have to keep
a wide distance from if I ever want to find

00:51:59.890 --> 00:52:06.759
the source of the transmission. Staying close
to the ocean floor, a curious, waving plant

00:52:06.759 --> 00:52:12.489
catches my eye. Swimming in for a closer look,
the lifeform lashes out with a long tendril

00:52:12.489 --> 00:52:18.110
and pulls me towards a mouth-like opening.
After a few frantic punches, the creature

00:52:18.110 --> 00:52:25.079
releases its tentacle and I swim out of range.
That was a Spikey Trap — which isn’t actually

00:52:25.079 --> 00:52:30.749
a plant at all, but a highly unusual form
of animal. This carnivorous lifeform blends

00:52:30.749 --> 00:52:36.489
into the terrain, camouflaging itself among
vegetation, and catching fish — and unwary

00:52:36.489 --> 00:52:42.329
explorers — with its adhesive tendrils when
they swim too close. And despite its lack

00:52:42.329 --> 00:52:49.070
of mobility, this strategy seems to be highly
effective. In the Deep Twisty Bridges, one

00:52:49.070 --> 00:52:56.099
must always stay alert. Elsewhere on the sea
floor, I discover a strange, glowing device

00:52:56.099 --> 00:53:01.880
near the signal’s source. This ruined fragment
matches the strange structures present in

00:53:01.880 --> 00:53:07.709
the tropics, which belonged to a group of
alien architects. I’d assumed all the architects

00:53:07.709 --> 00:53:13.150
had perished… but with the signal beckoning
me onwards, I wonder if I was mistaken…

00:53:13.150 --> 00:53:19.630
At last, I reach a mysterious undersea facility.
As I navigate through the halls, a mysterious

00:53:19.630 --> 00:53:26.069
voice urges me to hurry. Entering a central
chamber, I find the signal and the voice have

00:53:26.069 --> 00:53:30.970
been coming from this cube-shaped computer.
The voice turns out to be that of an architect

00:53:30.970 --> 00:53:36.619
who, thousands of years before, had stored
his consciousness within the computer. Speaking

00:53:36.619 --> 00:53:41.910
through the cube, the architect explains he
was a scientist like me, and is now likely

00:53:41.910 --> 00:53:47.329
the last of his kind. Wanting to help a fellow
researcher, I offer to transfer the lonely

00:53:47.329 --> 00:53:52.759
consciousness to my field computer and take
him with me on my expedition. The Architect

00:53:52.759 --> 00:53:59.000
accepts, and warns me to… brace for transfer?
A little too late, I realize something might

00:53:59.000 --> 00:54:04.359
have been lost in translation… Awakening
with a mild headache, I can hear the Architect’s

00:54:04.359 --> 00:54:09.730
voice quite clearly… and learn this is because
he downloaded himself directly into my brain.

00:54:09.730 --> 00:54:15.499
It was an honest mistake, as Architects don’t
recognize a boundary between technology and

00:54:15.499 --> 00:54:21.529
the body. While this is an intriguing revelation
about Architect biology, it means I now have

00:54:21.529 --> 00:54:28.829
an unanticipated mental roommate… Returning
to the shallows, we formulate a plan. We’ll

00:54:28.829 --> 00:54:33.589
use our combined knowledge to continue to
chart the biology of life in Sector Zero,

00:54:33.589 --> 00:54:38.329
but along the way we’ll gather the material
needed to fabricate a body that my new friend

00:54:38.329 --> 00:54:48.809
can transfer into. He tells me his designation
is A L dash A N… so I’ll call him Alan.

00:54:48.809 --> 00:54:53.119
Picking up where Alan’s research left off,
we venture to the sprawling expanse of the

00:54:53.119 --> 00:54:59.839
West Arctic. Here, great icebergs float above
a seemingly bottomless region of the sea — creating

00:54:59.839 --> 00:55:08.459
a profound sense of scale. And among these
icebergs swim undiscovered oddities. Gliding

00:55:08.459 --> 00:55:14.390
through the blue, is an animal that is difficult
to unravel. With two sets of wing-like fins,

00:55:14.390 --> 00:55:20.380
it’s not clear what sort of lifeform we’re
observing at a glance. But this lifeform is,

00:55:20.380 --> 00:55:26.359
in fact, an Arctic Ray, the first of many
Ray species in Sector Zero. While its body

00:55:26.359 --> 00:55:31.680
plan has clearly diverged from Earth rays,
by having four wing-structures instead of

00:55:31.680 --> 00:55:38.959
two, the Arctic Ray is a highly agile lifeform…
And it has to be, if it wants to avoid the

00:55:38.959 --> 00:55:45.170
top predator of the West Arctic. This is a
Pinnacarid, an intelligent and social predator

00:55:45.170 --> 00:55:51.099
that spends half its time on ice floats above
the water. Pinnacarids have converged on an

00:55:51.099 --> 00:55:56.539
ecological niche similar in many respects
to Earth seals, and are remarkably friendly

00:55:56.539 --> 00:56:03.059
towards humans, even showing acute curiosity
when lured in with a tasty piece of fish.

00:56:03.059 --> 00:56:08.699
Alan’s analysis suggests their fourteen
flippers make them acrobatic hunters underwater.

00:56:08.699 --> 00:56:14.690
This unusually high number of flippers resembles
the anatomy of the Anomalocarids, a group

00:56:14.690 --> 00:56:21.559
from Earth’s Cambrian Period who similarly
possessed multiple fins for locomotion. Descending

00:56:21.559 --> 00:56:27.989
deeper into the West Arctic waters, what appear
to be a legion of pulsating eyes stare back

00:56:27.989 --> 00:56:35.499
at us. This is, in fact, a colony of Eye Jellies,
gelatinous lifeforms that harbor an enormous

00:56:35.499 --> 00:56:42.069
hemispheric eye on the tops of their bodies.
Despite their alarming appearance, these creatures

00:56:42.069 --> 00:56:48.619
live remarkably passive lives, drifting slowly
on the currents in vast colonies, paying little

00:56:48.619 --> 00:56:54.269
mind to other species. Alan warns me that
they are capable of releasing a small electric

00:56:54.269 --> 00:57:01.289
shock if we swim too close, however, so we’ll
give the group a wide berth. Upon one of the

00:57:01.289 --> 00:57:07.849
West Arctic icebergs, we notice an entrance
to a small cave. Venturing inside, the reflected

00:57:07.849 --> 00:57:13.420
ice caught in the afternoon sun resembles
the brilliance of the night sky. And within

00:57:13.420 --> 00:57:20.250
this ethereal cave, a strange egg appears
stranded on the cave wall. It’s not a species

00:57:20.250 --> 00:57:26.859
Alan recognizes, and it seems about ready
to hatch. We bring the egg to the warmth of

00:57:26.859 --> 00:57:32.960
the shallows, after some time, this creature
emerges. The unusual hatchling turns out to

00:57:32.960 --> 00:57:38.839
be a Trivalve, and much like the Cuddlefish
of the tropics, it’s a remarkably friendly

00:57:38.839 --> 00:57:44.309
and intelligent creature that is quite a fan
of treats. A scan suggests the body of this

00:57:44.309 --> 00:57:50.289
naturally inquisitive creature is made of
a flexible exoskeleton, and somewhat resembles

00:57:50.289 --> 00:57:55.449
the shell of an earth Nautilus in its shape.
It’s tempting to relax in the shallows with

00:57:55.449 --> 00:58:03.119
the Trivalve, but Alan reminds me we still
have other areas to chart. The next leg of

00:58:03.119 --> 00:58:08.529
the journey will be perilous indeed. Luckily,
I have a new classification of submersible

00:58:08.529 --> 00:58:15.029
to aid in the voyage. This is a Sea Truck,
an underwater vehicle that will allow us to

00:58:15.029 --> 00:58:20.930
go lower into the crushing depths of Sector
Zero than ever before. At the helm of the

00:58:20.930 --> 00:58:27.229
Sea Truck, we venture into the vast biome
of the Thermal zone. The edge of this region,

00:58:27.229 --> 00:58:34.930
an area known as the Thermal Spires, is marked
by a forest of hydrothermal vents. These chimney-shaped

00:58:34.930 --> 00:58:40.130
structures are formed from dissolved minerals
pushed up from the planet’s crust, and are

00:58:40.130 --> 00:58:47.270
home to all kinds of remarkable life. Gliding
between the vents is a brightly-colored Feather

00:58:47.270 --> 00:58:53.170
Fish. Defined by their unusual, crescent-shaped
fin structure, Feather Fish are one of the

00:58:53.170 --> 00:58:59.049
more graceful herbivores in Sector Zero. A
highly successful species, in some regions

00:58:59.049 --> 00:59:06.529
they gather in schools numbering in the thousands.
We are admiring one such school, when an intimating

00:59:06.529 --> 00:59:12.249
creature paddles by. This vicious-looking,
armored predator is a Cryptosuchus, and is

00:59:12.249 --> 00:59:21.319
covered in sharp spines. Snapping wildly,
it closes in… then retreats just as quickly.

00:59:21.319 --> 00:59:26.869
As it turns out, the contrarian Cryptosuchus
is one species where its bark is truly worse

00:59:26.869 --> 00:59:32.549
than its bite. While it appears fearsome,
a scan suggests its bladed shell is adapted

00:59:32.549 --> 00:59:39.170
to aid in heat absorption among thermal vents.
And so, this false tyrant paddles away, to

00:59:39.170 --> 00:59:45.380
try and scare off something a bit smaller…
Far deeper into the Thermal Zone, the dark

00:59:45.380 --> 00:59:51.809
blue of a biome known as the Tree Spires beckons.
These waters are lit by the bioluminescent

00:59:51.809 --> 00:59:58.140
flora that grow on the sides of tree-like
hydrothermal vents. In this distinctive region,

00:59:58.140 --> 01:00:04.510
the equally distinctive Discus Fish ungulates
along. Its flat, semi-transparent body is

01:00:04.510 --> 01:00:11.509
highly unusual, and it seems to contain strange
green organs. A study of the Discus Fish reveals

01:00:11.509 --> 01:00:18.109
this green color comes from symbiotic algae-like
organisms living within specialized body cavities

01:00:18.109 --> 01:00:23.839
that provide the Discus Fish with food. Strange
as this might sound, Emerald Sea Slugs of

01:00:23.839 --> 01:00:29.670
Earth also photosynthesize using algae that
grow within their bodies. Since there isn’t

01:00:29.670 --> 01:00:34.239
much sunlight here in the depths for plant
growth, it’s likely the Discus Fish spends

01:00:34.239 --> 01:00:40.749
part of its lifecycle in a brighter region
of Sector Zero. Elsewhere in the Thermal Zone,

01:00:40.749 --> 01:00:47.380
we can spot a teardrop-shaped Arrow Ray. This
ray species has an elongated body and a rather

01:00:47.380 --> 01:00:53.609
triangular head, which it can tilt to perform
unpredictable maneuvers. At the ends of the

01:00:53.609 --> 01:00:58.759
Arrow Ray’s fins grow sharp, talon-like
tips that deter attackers who manage to catch

01:00:58.759 --> 01:01:06.239
up — meaning the Arrow Ray certainly isn’t
defenseless. A huge lifeform passes in front

01:01:06.239 --> 01:01:11.900
of our submarine. Without warning, it shrieks
and latches onto the windshield with its front

01:01:11.900 --> 01:01:15.069
mandibles, pulling us towards its gullet.
Gunning the engine, I’m barely able to twist

01:01:15.069 --> 01:01:20.859
out of the deadly grip. That leviathan class
organisms was a Chelicerate, a brute that

01:01:20.859 --> 01:01:27.849
grows over 130 feet, or 40 meters in length.
Its body is covered in a thick, segmented

01:01:27.849 --> 01:01:34.089
exoskeleton that grows in overlapping plates,
which may offer protection from the atmospheric

01:01:34.089 --> 01:01:39.749
pressure. Since the Chelicerate are so aggressive
and potentially deadly, they’re a challenging

01:01:39.749 --> 01:01:45.279
species to study up close. Fascinating as
they are, we both hope we don’t encounter

01:01:45.279 --> 01:01:52.479
any more of them. At the very bottom of the
sea floor, an indescribably massive lifeform

01:01:52.479 --> 01:01:59.380
is waiting. This is a Ventgarden, a stationary
leviathan that anchors itself above thermal

01:01:59.380 --> 01:02:07.359
hydrovents and consumes 100% of the nutrients
the vents release. At over 360 feet, or 110

01:02:07.359 --> 01:02:14.140
meters, the Ventgarden is, by far, the largest
Leviathan of Sector Zero. The only things

01:02:14.140 --> 01:02:19.459
in Earth’s Oceans that resemble Ventgardens
are certain types of siphonophores — soft

01:02:19.459 --> 01:02:25.380
bodied entities, which, like the Ventgardens,
are also technically colonies of smaller organisms

01:02:25.380 --> 01:02:31.339
working in tandem. But what’s most incredible
about the Ventgarden is the internal ecosystem

01:02:31.339 --> 01:02:37.880
of plants it supports within its hollow, bell-shaped
center. And remarkably, a scan indicates it

01:02:37.880 --> 01:02:43.980
would be safe to enter this environment ourselves.
Exiting the submarine, we tentatively swim

01:02:43.980 --> 01:02:52.049
up towards the light, and at last emerge within
the microcosm of the Ventgarden. This mini-ecosystem

01:02:52.049 --> 01:02:57.789
supports aquatic flora on branching platforms
that in turn help absorb some of the heavy

01:02:57.789 --> 01:03:04.140
metals emitted by the vents below. Exploring
the hidden biosphere of a Ventgarden is a

01:03:04.140 --> 01:03:12.209
downright magical experience… But the unknown
still beckons. Pushing further into the abyss

01:03:12.209 --> 01:03:18.089
than ever before, we’ve reached the edge
of the habitable region for most Fauna. Surrounded

01:03:18.089 --> 01:03:24.109
by total darkness, Alan is starting to get
worried that we’ve strayed too far. And

01:03:24.109 --> 01:03:30.150
he seems to be right. A huge Chelicerate emerges
from the blackness, almost twice the size

01:03:30.150 --> 01:03:35.900
as the one from the Tree Spires. This pale
mutation is called a Void Chelicerate, and

01:03:35.900 --> 01:03:42.839
it’s not alone. We’ve stumbled into a
pack. Time to head back as fast as we possibly

01:03:42.839 --> 01:03:49.270
can. I don’t think either of us plan on
revisiting this area any time soon. Returning

01:03:49.270 --> 01:03:55.030
to the safety of the research base, I have,
with Alan’s help, given our station a bit

01:03:55.030 --> 01:03:59.890
of an upgrade. As it turns out, having an
alien scientist downloaded into your brain

01:03:59.890 --> 01:04:04.789
can be quite useful. And while Alan still
doesn’t understand the purpose of basic

01:04:04.789 --> 01:04:10.410
things like music, or why I start every morning
with coffee, together, we’ve successfully

01:04:10.410 --> 01:04:15.579
mapped much of Sector Zero. Yet a deep scan
tells us that there’s a strange signature

01:04:15.579 --> 01:04:22.699
coming from a large ice cave we’ve yet to
chart… The cave is far to the inland of

01:04:22.699 --> 01:04:26.809
Sector Zero, across the frigid tundra. At
last, we enter the cavern and discover an

01:04:26.809 --> 01:04:33.230
enormous, frozen creature encased in a cave
wall. This leviathan class organism has been

01:04:33.230 --> 01:04:38.749
entombed here for thousands of years, as Alan
remembers encountering their kind when he

01:04:38.749 --> 01:04:45.150
was a researcher here long ago. Since this
species is, hopefully, now extinct, the only

01:04:45.150 --> 01:04:51.460
way to study it is to drill into the ice.
Taking a DNA sample from the creature’s

01:04:51.460 --> 01:04:56.739
foot, I’m able to determine the lifeform
was quadrupedal, and spent at least a portion

01:04:56.739 --> 01:05:04.719
of its time on land — something unusual
for planet 4546B. Perhaps this Frozen Leviathan

01:05:04.719 --> 01:05:10.509
suggests an era in the distant past where
land was more plentiful, and great titans

01:05:10.509 --> 01:05:16.309
like this one roamed across it. In any case,
I’m certainly glad this particular specimen

01:05:16.309 --> 01:05:23.920
is now long dead. I think. At long last, we
now have all the necessary materials needed

01:05:23.920 --> 01:05:30.290
to fabricate Alan’s new body. The only problem
is that the Architect Facility that can create

01:05:30.290 --> 01:05:36.849
such a compatible vessel is located in a region
known as the Crystal Caves. These caverns

01:05:36.849 --> 01:05:44.019
are the deepest biome of them all, and the
last frontier of planet 4546B. And since the

01:05:44.019 --> 01:05:49.269
refractive crystals mean any scans of the
region come back fragmented, there’s no

01:05:49.269 --> 01:05:55.729
telling what else is down there… Navigating
the purple chasm, this environment possesses

01:05:55.729 --> 01:06:03.539
a strange beauty. And equally strange lifeforms
navigate their depths. The Triops is a tiny

01:06:03.539 --> 01:06:08.489
lifeform that spends much of its time hiding
from predators in the surrounding terrain.

01:06:08.489 --> 01:06:14.430
The Triops stand out biologically, however,
thanks to their distinctive, three-eyed ocular

01:06:14.430 --> 01:06:20.660
system. In nature, a lifeform evolving three
distinct eyes isn’t particularly common…

01:06:20.660 --> 01:06:26.910
but it’s not altogether unheard of. Artemia
is a genus of aquatic crustaceans that possess

01:06:26.910 --> 01:06:32.939
two eyes mounted on flexible stalks and a
third, stationary eye situated in the center

01:06:32.939 --> 01:06:39.670
of their head. So, the diminutive Triops is
in good company. Further into the planet’s

01:06:39.670 --> 01:06:45.380
crust, we’ve arrived at the Fabricator Caves:
a secondary layer of the Crystal Caves, where

01:06:45.380 --> 01:06:52.069
a high concentration of the element Beryllium
led to the formation of striking red crystals.

01:06:52.069 --> 01:06:58.529
To brave this extreme geochemical environment,
I’ve donned the Prawn Suit: a deep-sea mechanical

01:06:58.529 --> 01:07:04.369
walker that kept me safe on the last stages
of my journey in the tropics. Stomping along

01:07:04.369 --> 01:07:11.519
the ocean floor, I spot a small crustacean
nearby. Given its diminutive stature, I’m

01:07:11.519 --> 01:07:18.180
not particularly worried, until it launches
itself at my Prawn Suit. This is a Rock Puncher,

01:07:18.180 --> 01:07:23.599
a pint-sized predator which is a heavy-hitter
nonetheless. Its hardened chitin claws can

01:07:23.599 --> 01:07:30.289
thrust forward at close to the speed of sound
to break rock and bone alike. In many respects,

01:07:30.289 --> 01:07:35.049
the Rock Puncher is similar to the precocious
Mantis Shrimp. These unsuspecting creatures

01:07:35.049 --> 01:07:40.910
possess spring-loaded biological hammer-clubs
that can strike prey faster than the speed

01:07:40.910 --> 01:07:46.130
of a bullet, giving the Mantis Shrimp the
ability to truly punch above its weight class.

01:07:46.130 --> 01:07:53.440
And the Rock Puncher is not different. Time
to deescalate the situation… by moving elsewhere.

01:07:53.440 --> 01:07:59.509
We’re now very close to the Fabrication
facility, when a thunderous roar shakes the

01:07:59.509 --> 01:08:07.710
cavern. A huge Leviathan grips my Prawn Suit
and pulls me towards a glowing gullet. After

01:08:07.710 --> 01:08:13.650
smacking it with my prawn-suit’s arms, the
nightmarish serpent temporarily drops me.

01:08:13.650 --> 01:08:20.900
Backing into a narrow gap, it seems the leviathan
cannot follow. We’re safe… for now. That

01:08:20.900 --> 01:08:28.219
was a Shadow Leviathan, which at almost 200
feet, or 60 meters, is the single most dangerous

01:08:28.219 --> 01:08:33.870
predator of Sector Zero. It’s black, elongated
body gives this monster the appearance of

01:08:33.870 --> 01:08:40.200
a giant eel. Analysis suggests its bioluminescent
digestive tract glows due to light seeking

01:08:40.200 --> 01:08:47.880
phytoplankton that draw in unwary fish, and
also secrets a highly acidic compound. I’m

01:08:47.880 --> 01:08:55.630
lucky I was able to escape such a terrifying
lifeform unscathed. Or have we? Looking up,

01:08:55.630 --> 01:09:01.040
I notice my Prawn Suit is leaking from its
fight with the Shadow Leviathan. Our only

01:09:01.040 --> 01:09:07.400
hope now is to reach the Fabrication facility
before the Shadow finds me again. But with

01:09:07.400 --> 01:09:14.390
the familiar green glow of Architect technology
up ahead, at last, we’ve reached the ultimate

01:09:14.390 --> 01:09:20.029
destination. Heading inside, I find a facility
quite like the one I first discovered Alan

01:09:20.029 --> 01:09:26.549
inside. Putting in the materials we’ve gathered,
the facility begins the process of fabrication.

01:09:26.549 --> 01:09:32.089
Activating the final sequence, Alan and I
watch as the vessel is constructed. This new

01:09:32.089 --> 01:09:38.589
body is composed of the DNA of 27 different
species, and various inorganic materials as

01:09:38.589 --> 01:09:44.739
well — so it should be quite the upgrade.
At last, Alan’s body is complete. After

01:09:44.739 --> 01:09:49.839
so much effort, it’s almost surreal to reach
this point. I think about telling my friend

01:09:49.839 --> 01:09:54.900
he looks a bit like a glowing purple centaur…
but decide that would probably just confuse

01:09:54.900 --> 01:10:00.630
him. With Alan now out of my mind and in his
new vessel, he doesn’t need my assistance

01:10:00.630 --> 01:10:06.690
to continue his research. We both return to
the surface, and Alan informs me he’ll be

01:10:06.690 --> 01:10:12.179
going back to his home planet via Warp Gate,
to see if there are any other Architects still

01:10:12.179 --> 01:10:18.530
living. It is a somewhat bittersweet feeling
as I realize this is goodbye. Working with

01:10:18.530 --> 01:10:25.059
another researcher — in particular an alien
one — has been a special privilege. Alone

01:10:25.059 --> 01:10:31.381
on Planet 4546B once more, I consider all
I’ve seen on this wondrous planet. From

01:10:31.381 --> 01:10:38.099
nerve-wracking dangers to unexpected allies,
it’s been a remarkable journey. But now,

01:10:38.099 --> 01:10:43.420
at last, the time has come for me to depart
as well. For who knows what else might be

01:10:43.420 --> 01:10:46.320
waiting for us among the stars…? If you
enjoyed discovering the creatures of Subnautica,

01:10:46.320 --> 01:10:47.760
please follow and support creature designer
Alex Ries using the links below. And as always,

01:10:47.760 --> 01:10:53.170
thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,
please lend your support by liking, subscribing,

01:10:53.170 --> 01:10:58.711
and hitting the notification icon to stay
up to date on all things Curious. See you

01:10:58.711 --> 01:10:59.999
in the next video.

